¶
'''tooth''' (töth), ''v.'' [< ME. ''toothen, tothen''; < ''tooth,'' ''n.''] I. ''trans.'' 1. To bite; taste.

{{smaller block|They were many times in doubt which they should ''touth'' first, or taste last. {{right|''Gosson,'' Schoole of Abuse.}}}}

2. To furnish with teeth: as, to ''tooth'' a rake.

{{smaller block|That towe is ''toothed'' thicke as the mesure
Of erees wol not passe hem, upwarde bende . . .
And every corne wol start into this chare.
{{right|''Palladius,'' Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 159.}}}}

{{smaller block|The twin cards ''toothed'' with glittering wire.
{{right|''Wordsworth.''}}}}

3. To indent; cut into teeth; jag.

{{smaller block|Then saws were ''toothed,'' and sounding axes made.
{{right|''Dryden,'' tr. of Virgil's Georgics, i. 215.}}}}

4. To lock one in another.

II. ''intrans.'' 1†. To teethe.

{{smaller block|When thaire crestes springe
As seke are thay as children in ''tothinge.''
{{right|''Palladius,'' Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 25.}}}}

2. To interlock, as cog-wheels.

'''toothache''' (töth′āk), ''n.'' [Formerly also ''toothach, toothake''; < ME. ''tothache,'' < AS. ''tōthece,'' < ''tōth,'' tooth, + ''ece,'' ache: see ''tooth'' and ''ache''<sup>1</sup>.] Pain in the teeth; odontalgia. Toothache was once supposed to be caused by a worm in the tooth. Compare ''worm.''

{{smaller block|Coughes and cardiacles, crampes and ''tothaches.''
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), xx. 81.}}}}

{{smaller block|I am troubled
With the ''toothache,'' or with love, I know not whether;
There is a worm in both.
{{right|''Massinger,'' Parliament of Love, i. 5.}}}}

